---
import IllustrationFrame from './IllustrationFrame.astro';
import { payrollIllustration } from '../lib/artifacts/payroll-rule.mjs';

const r = payrollIllustration();
const days = (n: number) => n.toFixed(1);
const pct = (n: number) => `${Math.round(n * 100)}%`;
const { earliest, frequent } = r.evaluation;
---
<IllustrationFrame
  title="Why the date rule matters, shown on made-up payroll."
  purpose="It compares the two payroll-date rules from the table above on invented payments."
>
  <table class="decision-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Average miss (days)</th><th>Forecast exactly right</th><th>Months scored</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {[earliest, frequent].map((e) => (
        <tr><td>{e.label}</td><td>{days(e.mae)}</td><td>{pct(e.exactShare)}</td><td>{e.n}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Each month is forecast using only the {r.window} months before it. The gap depends on how often a one-off early
    payment happens, so here is the same test with different assumptions:
  </p>
  <table class="decision-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>One-off early payments</th><th>Earliest rule (days)</th><th>Most frequent rule (days)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {r.sensitivity.map((s) => (
        <tr><td>{pct(s.probability)} of months</td><td>{days(s.earliestMae)}</td><td>{days(s.frequentMae)}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The most frequent rule is not perfect either. When the payday falls on a weekend the payment moves earlier, and a
    rule that only looks at past dates cannot see the calendar.
  </p>
  <Fragment slot="method">
    <p>
      {r.history.length} invented entities, {r.months} months each. Every entity has a contract payday between the 14th
      and 28th. If it falls on a weekend, payment moves to the Friday before. In {pct(r.oneOffEarlyProbability)} of
      months a one-off run pays 3 to 6 days early. Code: <code>src/lib/artifacts/payroll-rule.mjs</code>, tests in
      <code>scripts/artifacts.test.mjs</code>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFrame>
